' Title
title Basic Observer Pattern - Core Structure

' Subject interface
interface Subject <<Subject>> {
  +attach(observer: Observer): void
  +detach(observer: Observer): void
  +notifyObservers(): void
}

' Observer interface
interface Observer <<Observer>> {
  +update(subject: Subject): void
}

' ConcreteSubject
class ConcreteSubject <<ConcreteSubject>> {
  -observers: List<Observer>
  -state: Object
  +attach(observer: Observer): void
  +detach(observer: Observer): void
  +notifyObservers(): void
  +getState(): Object
  +setState(state: Object): void
}

' ConcreteObservers
class ConcreteObserverA <<ConcreteObserver>> {
  -observerState: Object
  +update(subject: Subject): void
}

class ConcreteObserverB <<ConcreteObserver>> {
  -observerState: Object
  +update(subject: Subject): void
}

class ConcreteObserverC <<ConcreteObserver>> {
  -observerState: Object
  +update(subject: Subject): void
}

' Client
class Client <<Client>> {
  +main(): void
}

' Relationships
Subject <|.. ConcreteSubject
Observer <|.. ConcreteObserverA
Observer <|.. ConcreteObserverB
Observer <|.. ConcreteObserverC

ConcreteSubject o--> "0..*" Observer : observers
ConcreteSubject ..> Observer : notifies >

Client ..> Subject : creates/uses >
Client ..> Observer : creates/attaches >

' Pattern component notes
note right of Subject
  <b>Subject (Observable)</b>

  Defines the interface for attaching,
  detaching, and notifying observers.

  <b>Responsibilities:</b>
  • Maintain list of observers
  • Provide methods to add/remove observers
  • Define notification mechanism

  <b>Key Methods:</b>
  • attach() - register an observer
  • detach() - unregister an observer
  • notifyObservers() - inform all observers

  Subjects don't need to know concrete
  observer types - only the Observer interface.
end note

note left of Observer
  <b>Observer</b>

  Defines the callback interface for
  objects that should be notified of
  changes in the subject.

  <b>Responsibilities:</b>
  • Define update/notification method
  • Respond to subject state changes

  <b>Update Method:</b>
  Called by subject when state changes.
  Observer pulls data from subject or
  receives it as parameters (push/pull model).

  Multiple observers can watch the
  same subject independently.
end note

note right of ConcreteSubject
  <b>ConcreteSubject</b>

  Implements the Subject interface and
  maintains state that observers are interested in.

  <b>Responsibilities:</b>
  • Store state that observers care about
  • Implement observer management (attach/detach)
  • Notify observers when state changes
  • Provide state access methods (for pull model)

  <b>State Change Flow:</b>
  1. setState(newState) is called
  2. Internal state is updated
  3. notifyObservers() is called
  4. Each observer's update() is invoked

  <b>Implementation Details:</b>
  • Maintains List<Observer> for registered observers
  • Iterates through observers during notification
  • May include duplicate prevention logic
  • Should handle exceptions from faulty observers
end note

note left of ConcreteObserverA
  <b>ConcreteObserver A</b>

  Implements the Observer interface
  with specific update logic.

  <b>Responsibilities:</b>
  • Implement update() method
  • Maintain reference to subject (optional)
  • Store observer-specific state
  • React to subject changes

  <b>Update Implementation:</b>
  public void update(Subject subject) {
    Object newState = subject.getState();
    // React to the change
    this.observerState = newState;
    // Perform observer-specific actions
  }

  Each observer can respond differently
  to the same subject change.
end note

note right of Client
  <b>Client</b>

  Creates subjects and observers,
  establishes their relationships.

  <b>Typical Usage:</b>
  Subject subject = new ConcreteSubject();
  Observer obs1 = new ConcreteObserverA();
  Observer obs2 = new ConcreteObserverB();

  subject.attach(obs1);
  subject.attach(obs2);

  subject.setState(newState);
  // Automatically notifies obs1 and obs2

  Client doesn't manage notifications -
  the pattern handles that automatically.
end note

' Pattern flow note
note bottom of ConcreteSubject
  <b>Observer Pattern Flow:</b>

  1. Client creates Subject and Observers
  2. Client attaches Observers to Subject via attach()
  3. Subject's state changes (setState() called)
  4. Subject calls notifyObservers()
  5. Subject iterates through observer list
  6. Subject calls update() on each Observer
  7. Each Observer retrieves new state (pull) or receives it (push)
  8. Each Observer reacts to the change independently

  <b>Key Advantages:</b>
  • Subject and Observers are loosely coupled
  • One-to-many dependency automatically maintained
  • Observers can be added/removed dynamically
  • No tight coupling between Subject and concrete Observers

  <b>Push vs. Pull Model:</b>

  PUSH: Subject sends data to observers
  update(data: Object)

  PULL: Observers query subject for data
  update(subject: Subject)
  then observer calls subject.getState()

  Pull model shown here - more flexible.
end note

' Legend
note bottom of Observer
  <b>Observer Pattern Core Components:</b>

  <b>Subject:</b> The object being observed. Maintains
  a list of observers and notifies them of state changes.

  <b>Observer:</b> Interface for objects that want to be
  notified of subject changes. Defines the update callback.

  <b>ConcreteSubject:</b> Implements Subject interface.
  Stores state of interest to observers and sends
  notifications when state changes.

  <b>ConcreteObserver:</b> Implements Observer interface.
  Maintains reference to ConcreteSubject (optional) and
  defines specific response to notifications.

  <b>Key Benefits:</b>
  • Loose coupling between subject and observers
  • Dynamic observer registration/deregistration
  • Broadcast communication (one-to-many)
  • Open/Closed Principle (add new observers without changing subject)
  • Supports multiple independent views of data
  • Automatic notification mechanism

  <b>Common Use Cases:</b>
  • Event handling systems
  • Model-View-Controller (MVC) pattern
  • Publish/Subscribe messaging
  • Data binding in UI frameworks
  • Monitoring and logging systems

  <b>Intent:</b>
  Define a one-to-many dependency between objects so
  that when one object changes state, all its dependents
  are notified and updated automatically.

  <b>Real-World Analogy:</b>
  Like subscribing to a newsletter. When the publisher
  (subject) releases new content, all subscribers
  (observers) are automatically notified. Subscribers
  can join or leave the mailing list at any time.
end note

@enduml